What that *MEANS*, Mr. Read, is that there is a system of simple things with
a few rules to govern their interaction. So *TAKE*, for example, Mr. Read,
the game of life. There is a grid with simple rules defining the interaction
of the binary state of each cell. The structure is very simple, however, the
results are very complicated. In fact, the matematician Stephen Wolfram
recently went crazy and wrote a book where he espoused the idea that
cellular automata (the general term for games of life) can be used to
describe Life, the Universe, and Everything.

Another good example is language. Although it's not as simple as the game of
life, there is a finite set of words and grammatical rules. However, there
is no theoretical end to the number of new sentences that can be built,
given enough paper, or breath.

I *GUESS* to some extent you can *SAY* that the current NH grid exhibits
this kind of complexity. Each monster is allowed your 9 arrow key movements,
and given a large enough room, you could have unlimited configuration of
monsters. That's kind of boring, though.

I was trying to get some creative thinking started more along the lines of
the magic system or the potion system. See if you can't break it down into a
set of simpler rules, somehow, that can provide all the existing
functionality. Consider this a call for a brainstorming session. So
currently magic works like

if zap wand_of_ice
if target is fire_creature
then double_damage;

Even for something like a towel, which has a million and one uses, each
feature is an if statement
if pie_in_face
if apply_towel
then face_clean;

if apply_towel
then blind;

So all you have is just a big laundry list of features. It's deterministic;
nothing new or surprising will happen. If you find a new feature, it's
because someone thought it up and put it in this, not because it arises out
of the system. You're catching other people's cleverness, not making any
discoveries.

Let me re-iterate, I'm not trying to knock the current game, and I know the
DevTeam puts a lot of hard work in this. But I think it would be cool if we
could implement something like a discrete combinatorial system (a system
like language) for spells or potions.

So, if you have any ideas, half-ideas,  foggy notions, pipe up. If you have
criticisms, feel free, but what's the point?

